\section*{Significance Statement}

The UPAS-X engine introduces a new class of high-precision astrophysical simulation 
capable of modeling multi-body shadow geometry, relativistic ephemerides, and 
high-dynamic-range flux fields at billion-point spatial scales. By integrating a 
15th-order symplectic ephemeris solver, analytic ray--cone geometry, and 
distributed BVH spatial partitioning across a large-scale GPU cluster, UPAS-X 
achieves numerical stability and computational throughput beyond the limits of 
existing eclipse solvers, photometric pipelines, and ray-tracing frameworks.

UPAS-X demonstrates sub-kilometer accuracy in reconstructing historical eclipse 
paths, validates orbital trajectories against NASA JPL HORIZONS ephemerides, and 
produces flux attenuation profiles consistent with Gaia and Kepler limb-darkening 
coefficients. These results establish UPAS-X as a physically accurate and 
empirically grounded simulation platform.

The engine’s deterministic reproducibility—enabled by cryptographic seed locking, 
immutable configuration manifests, and rigorous failure-mode monitoring—sets a 
new methodological standard for large-scale astrophysical computation. UPAS-X 
therefore represents a field-defining advance, providing a foundation for 
next-generation research in eclipse prediction, exoplanet transit modeling, 
stellar photometry, and multi-body dynamical systems.